module Tool.Env exposing (Model, Step, command, init, menu, menuItem, update, view, windowId)

{-| Every environment variable predc's own process has, one per line, name and
value.

The thing a person actually wants when they ask "is `NO_COLOR` set in here?" or
"what did tmux do to `TERM`?" -- questions that are one `env | grep` away in a
shell and, over ssh with three windows already open, further away than that
sounds.

**It is a snapshot from before the first frame, and there is no Refresh.**
`Node.getEnvironmentVariables` is an `Init.Task`, so the shell reads it once on
the way in and hands it here. That is not a limitation worked around: a
process's environment is only changed by the process itself, and predc never
changes its own, so what is on the screen cannot go stale. A Refresh would
redraw the same list and quietly imply that it might not.

**The search box filters rather than jumps.** `n`-for-next is the right shape
for a hex dump, where the answer is a position in something too big to see; a
list of forty names is not that. Typing `proxy` and being shown the four
variables with `proxy` in them *is* the question, and the status line says
`4 of 47` so nothing is hidden without a count. Emptying the box brings
everything back.

Matching is case-insensitive by default and the check box says so, because a
person hunting for `http_proxy` should not have to know whether this machine
spells it in capitals. The needle is matched against `NAME=value`, so `PATH=`
finds the variable and not the twenty others that mention a path.

**The values are bytes, and predc reads them as bytes.** `process.env` is
`Dict String String` and Node decoded it: a value that is not valid UTF-8
arrives as one `U+FFFD` per byte and the bytes are gone for good. That is not
hypothetical -- a `LANG` of `ko_KR.euc-kr` and a path with a Korean directory
in it is somebody's ordinary Tuesday -- and the list was showing replacement
characters with nothing to say that is what they were. So `Main` reads
`/proc/self/environ`, the block `execve` handed the process, and hands it here.
[`Value`](#Value) is what came of that: a value either decodes, or it does not
and only its bytes are true, or there was no `/proc` on this machine and predc
is repeating what Node told it.

A value that does not decode is drawn the way a hex dump draws its printable
column -- the ASCII as itself and everything else as `.` -- in the alert ink,
so a path with one Korean directory in it still reads as a path with a run of
dots in the middle. Which is where the trouble is, and not what it is.

**`x` is what answers that**, and it is why this tool has a cursor. It sends
the value under the cursor to the hex viewer, which already knows how to hold
bytes that were never a file. The list scrolled and did not select before,
because filtering was the whole interaction and nothing needed one variable
rather than the set; a byte view needs exactly one, and `Up`/`Down` move over
*variables* rather than display lines so that a wrapped `PATH` is one stop and
not nine.

Once there is a cursor, `y` means the value under it -- the value alone, since
a copy of one is a thing about to be pasted where the value goes. `Y` is what
`y` used to be, every shown variable as `NAME=value`, which is still worth
having after a filter and is still written whole because a list of bare values
says nothing about which is which.

-}

import ByteArray
import Bytes exposing (Bytes)
import Bytes.Encode as Encode
import Dict exposing (Dict)
import Theme exposing (Inks)
import Tui exposing (MenuItem(..), Span, View(..))


-- NAMES


command : String
command =
    "tool.env"


windowId : String
windowId =
    "env"


findId : String
findId =
    "env.find"


caseId : String
caseId =
    "env.case"


listId : String
listId =
    "env.list"


scrollId : String
scrollId =
    "env.bar"


{-| `Alt-P`, for the **p**rocess's environment -- and because it was one of two
letters left.

The count is worth writing down, because this file corrects it. `Tool.Calendar`
and `Tool.Random` both say the free letters were `J`, `K`, `Q` and `V`, which
was measured by looking for `~x~` anywhere in the sources -- and that is too
strict by a wide margin. `TMenuView::findHotKey` matches `p->keyCode`, the
accelerator, and **not** the underlined letter in an item's title
(`tmnuview.cpp:557`); an entry inside a pull-down claims its letter only while
that pull-down is on the screen. What actually claims an `Alt` key is a menu
*bar* title, an item's `key`, and a caption on a view in an open window.

Counted that way, `E`, `P` and `Q` were free. `E` would have been the obvious
one and is not free after all: `Tool.Unicode`'s menu is `~E~ncoding`, which is
on the bar whenever the decoder is open. So `P`, and `Q` is what is left for
whatever comes next.

The underlined letter is a different question and a different namespace. It was
the `v` of *En~v~ironment*, which `Random ~v~alues` -- the entry directly above
it -- already had, so it opened the wrong tool; it is the `E` now, which is
free among the nine entries of that one pull-down and claims nothing outside
it.
-}
menuItem : MenuItem
menuItem =
    Item { title = "~E~nvironment variables", cmd = command, key = "Alt-P", shortcut = "Alt-P" }


{-| The tool's own menu, and **its title has no underlined letter on purpose**.

Every letter of *Search* is spoken for by something that can be on the screen at
the same time: `S` is the converter's `~S~top clock` button, `e` is the
decoder's `~E~ncoding` menu, `a` the ASCII chart, `r` the calculator, `c` the
converter and `h` Help. A menu bar title beats a button silently, so underlining
any of them would take a key away from another window without a word.

A caption with no `~` in it claims nothing -- the same rule `Tool.Random`'s
buttons use, applied to a bar title. The menu still opens with the mouse and
with `F10`, and everything on it has a one-letter key on the list itself.
-}
menu : MenuItem
menu =
    SubMenu
        { title = "Search"
        , key = ""
        , items =
            [ Item { title = "~F~ind box", cmd = "env.find", key = "", shortcut = "/" }
            , Item { title = "Match ~c~ase", cmd = "env.case", key = "", shortcut = "c" }
            , Item { title = "Show them a~l~l", cmd = "env.all", key = "", shortcut = "" }
            , Tui.Separator
            , Item { title = "Cop~y~ this value", cmd = "env.copy", key = "", shortcut = "y" }
            , Item { title = "Copy ~e~verything shown", cmd = "env.copyall", key = "", shortcut = "Y" }
            , Tui.Separator
            , Item { title = "Send value to he~x~ dump", cmd = "env.hex", key = "", shortcut = "x" }
            ]
        }


-- MODEL


type alias Pair =
    { name : String, value : Value }


{-| What the tool knows about one value, which is not always what it says.

`process.env` is a `Dict String String`, and Node decoded it: a value that is
not valid UTF-8 arrives as one `U+FFFD` per byte, irrecoverably. A `LANG` of
`ko_KR.euc-kr` and a value with EUC-KR bytes in it is a real thing to have, and
the list was showing four replacement characters and nothing to say that is
what they were. Worse, "send this to the hex viewer" built on that string would
have shown `EF BF BD` repeated -- the encoding of the damage rather than the
bytes.

So predc reads `/proc/self/environ` itself, which is the block `execve` handed
the process, byte for byte. `Bytes.toString` is the whole decoder: it is
`TextDecoder` with `fatal: true`, so `Nothing` *is* the answer to "can this be
shown as text", asked once per value with no decoder of our own.

  - `Exact` -- the bytes decode, and the string is the whole truth about them.
  - `Opaque` -- they do not, so only the bytes are true. Shown the way a hex
    dump shows its printable column, and in the alert ink, because a row that
    cannot be read has to look different from one that can.
  - `Reported` -- there was no byte source on this machine, so this is
    `process.env`'s word for it and predc cannot vouch for it. `/proc` is
    Linux; a mac has no equivalent a program can read.

**The third one is why this is a union and not a `Maybe (Array Int)`.**
"I have no bytes" and "the bytes say nothing printable" are different facts,
and only the first of them means the hex viewer cannot be offered.
-}
type Value
    = Exact String
    | Opaque (Array Int)
    | Reported String


{-| What a row shows.

An `Opaque` value is rendered the way `Tool.Hex` renders its printable column:
a byte outside `0x20..0x7E` becomes `.`, so a path with one Korean directory in
it still reads as a path, with a run of dots where the bytes are. Hiding the
whole value behind a byte count would answer a question nobody asked.
-}
shownAs : Value -> String
shownAs value =
    when value is
        Exact it ->
            it

        Reported it ->
            it

        Opaque bytes ->
            Array.map
                (\byte ->
                    if byte >= 0x20 && byte <= 0x7E then
                        String.fromChar (Char.fromCode byte)

                    else
                        "."
                )
                bytes
                |> String.join ""


{-| The bytes to hand the hex viewer, or `Nothing` when there are none to hand.

`Exact` re-encodes rather than storing a second copy: the string came from
those bytes and UTF-8 is a round trip, so the two cannot disagree.
-}
bytesOf : Value -> Maybe (Array Int)
bytesOf value =
    when value is
        Exact it ->
            Just (ByteArray.fromString it)

        Opaque bytes ->
            Just bytes

        Reported _ ->
            Nothing


{-| Whether the string on the screen is the whole truth about the value. -}
readable : Value -> Bool
readable value =
    when value is
        Opaque _ ->
            False

        _ ->
            True


type alias Model =
    { -- Every variable, sorted by name, exactly as the process was handed it.
      -- Never written to again: see the note at the top about Refresh.
      all : Array Pair
    , needle : String
    , matchCase : Bool
    -- The first *display line* on screen, not the first variable. A value
    -- longer than the window wraps onto continuation lines, so the two are
    -- different numbers and scrolling is about the one you can see.
    , top : Int
    -- Which variable is under the cursor, as an index into the *filtered*
    -- list -- `shown model`, not `all`. A variable and not a display line,
    -- which is the whole of the difference between a cursor and a scroll
    -- position: `Down` goes to the next variable, not to the next wrapped
    -- line of the one you are already on. The lines follow it, not the other
    -- way round.
    --
    -- It is clamped rather than remembered when the filter moves, because a
    -- needle that shortens the list has no opinion about which of the
    -- survivors you meant, and the one honest answer at that moment is the
    -- top of what is left.
    , cursor : Int
    -- What the window was built at, and never changed afterwards. The hex
    -- viewer says why a rectangle is normally *not* stored -- what the model
    -- renders is what the differ compares against, so a rectangle that moves
    -- is a `setBounds` written over wherever the user last put the window --
    -- and the same rule is what makes storing it safe here: it is computed
    -- once from the desktop and is a constant from then on. Turbo Vision does
    -- the rest, since a window built here is `gfGrowAll | gfGrowRel` and
    -- follows the terminal on its own.
    , rect : Tui.Rect
    -- How wide and how tall the canvas is *now*, from `WindowResized`. Two
    -- numbers rather than one because both of them are used: the height
    -- decides how much of the list is on screen, and the width decides where a
    -- long value wraps.
    , cols : Int
    , rows : Int
    , copied : Bool
    , message : String
    }


type alias Step msg =
    { model : Model, command : Cmd msg, send : Maybe Handoff }


{-| Bytes on their way to the hex viewer, which is the one thing this tool
cannot do for itself.

A field on the step rather than a `Cmd`, because the shell is what owns the
other tool: a `Cmd` can ask the runtime for something, and opening a window
belonging to `Tool.Hex` is not the runtime's to give. `Main` already reads
every tool's step to take its model; this is one more field on the same read.

`name` is what the viewer's title bar will say. It is written `$LS_COLORS`
rather than `LS_COLORS` so that a dump on screen says what kind of thing it is
looking at, which is the same reason the viewer never guesses.
-}
type alias Handoff =
    { name : String, bytes : Array Int }


{-| The widest a name column gets before the values start losing room.

Names are usually short and occasionally not -- `LESS_TERMCAP_us` and
`npm_package_json` are real -- and a single outlier should not push every value
twenty columns right. Past this the name is written whole anyway and the value
starts one space after it, out of line with the rest. Out of line is honest;
cut off would not be.

A third of the window rather than a fixed number, because the point of it is
the *share* of the row the names may have, and a window can now be any width.
-}
namesAtMost : Int -> Int
namesAtMost wide =
    clamp 12 40 (wide // 3)


{-| The window fills the desktop.

Not a default that happens to be about right: this tool is a list of everything
and the only question it ever asks of a layout is how much of it fits, so every
row the terminal has is a row worth having. The rectangle is worked out once,
here, from the size the shell was told -- which is why `Main` seeds that from
`Terminal.initialize` rather than from eighty-by-twenty-five, since `init` runs
before any `Resized` can have arrived.

Both dimensions are then free: `Tui.resizable`, and the canvas and the scroll
bar follow the frame. A long `PATH` wraps at whatever width the window is, so a
wide terminal is worth having too -- which is the other half of the same
complaint.
-}
init : Maybe Bytes -> Dict String String -> { cols : Int, rows : Int } -> Model
init environ vars desktop =
    let
        rect =
            { x1 = 0
            , y1 = 0
            , x2 = max 44 desktop.cols
            , y2 = max 9 desktop.rows
            }
    in
    { all =
        when environ is
            Just block ->
                parse (ByteArray.fromBytes block)

            Nothing ->
                Dict.foldl
                    (\name value sofar ->
                        Array.pushLast { name = name, value = Reported value } sofar
                    )
                    []
                    vars
    , needle = ""
    , matchCase = False
    , top = 0
    , cursor = 0
    , rect = rect
    , cols = colsIn (rect.x2 - rect.x1)
    , rows = rowsIn (rect.y2 - rect.y1)
    , copied = False
    , message = ""
    }


{-| The environment block, which is `NAME=value` records separated by NUL.

Three things about the format are worth writing down, because none of them is
guessable and all three are load bearing.

**Only the first `=` separates.** A value may contain as many as it likes --
`LESS_TERMCAP_se` and any `FOO=BAR=BAZ` a shell was asked to export -- and a
record with no `=` at all is not a variable and is dropped. `execve` does not
promise otherwise.

**There is a trailing NUL**, so splitting on it leaves an empty last record.
Dropping empty records handles that and costs nothing else, since a record with
no name is not a variable either.

**A name may repeat.** `execve` takes an array and nothing dedups it; `getenv`
answers the *first*, so this keeps the first and drops the rest -- which is what
the program whose environment this is would see, and therefore the only honest
answer. `process.env` dedups the other way round on some platforms, which is
one more reason not to use it as the source.

The name is decoded the same way the value is and for the same reason. A name
that is not UTF-8 is not a thing anybody has, but "not a thing anybody has" is
not a reason for a list to be wrong about it.
-}
parse : Array Int -> Array Pair
parse block =
    splitOn 0 block
        |> Array.mapAndFlatten
            (\record ->
                when Array.findFirst (\byte -> byte == equals) record is
                    Nothing ->
                        []

                    Just found ->
                        let
                            at =
                                found.index

                            name =
                                Array.takeFirst at record
                        in
                        if Array.isEmpty name then
                            []

                        else
                            [ { name = shownAs (valueOf name)
                              , value = valueOf (Array.dropFirst (at + 1) record)
                              }
                            ]
            )
        |> Array.foldl
            (\pair sofar ->
                if Array.any (\seen -> seen.name == pair.name) sofar then
                    sofar

                else
                    Array.pushLast pair sofar
            )
            []
        |> Array.sortBy .name


equals : Int
equals =
    0x3D


{-| `Bytes.toString` is `TextDecoder` with `fatal: true`, so this whole
question is one call: a `Just` means the bytes are text and the string is all
of what they say, and a `Nothing` means only the bytes are.
-}
valueOf : Array Int -> Value
valueOf bytes =
    when Bytes.toString (bytesFrom bytes) is
        Just it ->
            Exact it

        Nothing ->
            Opaque bytes


bytesFrom : Array Int -> Bytes
bytesFrom bytes =
    Array.map (\byte -> Encode.unsignedInt8 byte) bytes
        |> Encode.sequence
        |> Encode.encode


splitOn : Int -> Array Int -> Array (Array Int)
splitOn sep bytes =
    Array.foldl
        (\byte sofar ->
            if byte == sep then
                Array.pushLast [] sofar

            else
                when Array.last sofar is
                    Just current ->
                        Array.set
                            (Array.length sofar - 1)
                            (Array.pushLast byte current)
                            sofar

                    Nothing ->
                        [ [ byte ] ]
        )
        [ [] ]
        bytes
        |> Array.keepIf (\record -> not (Array.isEmpty record))


-- WHAT IS SHOWN


{-| The variables the needle picks out, in the order they were in.

An empty needle picks out everything, which is what makes the filter feel like
a filter rather than a mode: there is no "searching" state to leave.
-}
shown : Model -> Array Pair
shown model =
    if String.isEmpty model.needle then
        model.all

    else
        Array.keepIf (matches model) model.all


matches : Model -> Pair -> Bool
matches model pair =
    let
        subject =
            pair.name ++ "=" ++ shownAs pair.value
    in
    if model.matchCase then
        String.contains model.needle subject

    else
        String.contains (String.toLower model.needle) (String.toLower subject)


{-| How wide the name column is: the widest name being shown, within reason.

The widest name *being shown* rather than the widest there is, so filtering to
one variable does not leave it sitting behind twenty spaces somebody else
needed.
-}
column : Int -> Array Pair -> Int
column wide pairs =
    pairs
        |> Array.map (\pair -> String.count pair.name)
        |> Array.maximum
        |> Maybe.withDefault 1
        |> clamp 1 (namesAtMost wide)


{-| Every variable as the lines it takes up.

A value wider than the window wraps to the next line, indented to the value
column, rather than being cut off at the frame -- which is the thing this
program keeps finding fixed spaces doing in silence. `PATH` is six lines here
and that is the correct number of lines for `PATH`.
-}
laidOut : Int -> Array Pair -> Array Line
laidOut width pairs =
    let
        wide =
            column width pairs

        room =
            max 8 (width - wide - 2)
    in
    Array.indexedMap (\at pair -> { at = at, pair = pair }) pairs
        |> Array.mapAndFlatten
            (\it ->
                let
                    gap =
                        max 1 ((wide + 2) - String.count it.pair.name)

                    indent =
                        String.repeat (wide + 2) " "

                    first rest =
                        it.pair.name ++ String.repeat gap " " ++ rest
                in
                when folded room (shownAs it.pair.value) is
                    [] ->
                        [ { at = it.at, readable = True, text = first "" } ]

                    chunks ->
                        Array.indexedMap
                            (\index chunk ->
                                { at = it.at
                                , readable = readable it.pair.value
                                , text =
                                    if index == 0 then
                                        first chunk

                                    else
                                        indent ++ chunk
                                }
                            )
                            chunks
            )


{-| One row of the canvas.

`at` is the index of the variable this line belongs to, and it is what makes a
cursor over *variables* drawable on a canvas of *lines* -- the highlight is
every line whose `at` is the cursor's, however many the value wrapped onto, and
the scroll only has to find the first of them.

`readable` is carried here rather than looked up again because `display` has
the line and not the pair, and asking a second time would mean matching on the
name -- which is a key, but a key nothing else in this file needs.
-}
type alias Line =
    { at : Int, readable : Bool, text : String }


folded : Int -> String -> Array String
folded room text =
    let
        go rest acc =
            if String.count rest <= room then
                Array.pushLast rest acc

            else
                go (String.dropFirst room rest)
                    (Array.pushLast (String.takeFirst room rest) acc)
    in
    if String.isEmpty text then
        [ "" ]

    else
        go text []


-- UPDATE


update : Tui.Ports msg -> Tui.Event -> Model -> Step msg
update ports event model =
    when event is
        Tui.WindowResized it ->
            if it.id == windowId then
                still
                    (scrollTo model.top
                        { model
                            | cols = colsIn (it.rect.x2 - it.rect.x1)
                            , rows = rowsIn (it.rect.y2 - it.rect.y1)
                        }
                    )

            else
                still model

        Tui.Changed it ->
            if it.id == findId then
                when it.value is
                    Tui.Text text ->
                        -- Back to the top on every keystroke, because the
                        -- list underneath has changed and neither a scroll
                        -- position nor a cursor into the old one means
                        -- anything. Keeping the cursor on the same *variable*
                        -- where it survives the filter was considered and
                        -- dropped: it makes the highlight jump around the
                        -- screen as you type, and the row you are aiming at is
                        -- the one you are typing to reach.
                        still { model | needle = text, top = 0, cursor = 0, message = "" }

                    _ ->
                        still model

            else if it.id == caseId then
                when it.value is
                    Tui.Flags ticks ->
                        still
                            { model
                                | matchCase = Maybe.withDefault False (Array.first ticks)
                                , top = 0
                                , cursor = 0
                                , message = ""
                            }

                    _ ->
                        still model

            else
                still model

        Tui.Scrolled bar ->
            if bar.id == scrollId then
                still (scrollTo bar.value model)

            else
                still model

        Tui.KeyPressed pressed ->
            if pressed.id == listId then
                pressKey ports pressed.key { model | message = "" }

            else
                still model

        -- A click puts the cursor on whatever was clicked, which is the only
        -- thing a click here means: the row under the pointer is a *line*, and
        -- the line knows which variable it belongs to, so a click on the third
        -- wrapped line of a long `PATH` selects `PATH` and not a fragment.
        --
        -- Two clicks and not one, and that is Turbo Vision rather than this:
        -- the first click on a canvas that does not hold the selection is
        -- spent giving it the selection. A window with a scroll bar has two
        -- things to point at, and one click that both moved the selection and
        -- acted would act in a view the user had not been working in.
        Tui.Clicked spot ->
            if spot.id == listId then
                when Array.get (model.top + spot.y) (laidOut model.cols (shown model)) is
                    Just line ->
                        still (moveTo line.at { model | message = "" })

                    Nothing ->
                        still model

            else
                still model

        Tui.Copied it ->
            if model.copied then
                still
                    { model
                        | copied = False
                        , message =
                            if it.toSystem then
                                "Copied to the clipboard."

                            else
                                "Copied; F1 if it does not paste."
                    }

            else
                still model

        Tui.Command cmd ->
            when cmd is
                "env.find" ->
                    { model = { model | message = "" }
                    , command = Tui.focus ports findId
                    , send = Nothing
                    }

                "env.case" ->
                    still
                        { model
                            | matchCase = not model.matchCase
                            , top = 0
                            , cursor = 0
                            , message = ""
                        }

                "env.all" ->
                    still { model | needle = "", top = 0, cursor = 0, message = "" }

                "env.copy" ->
                    copyValue ports model

                "env.copyall" ->
                    copyShown ports model

                "env.hex" ->
                    sendToHex model

                _ ->
                    still model

        _ ->
            still model


{-| The keys the list answers to.

`/` is the one worth explaining: it moves the caret to the search box. A
focused canvas consumes every key it is given -- `JsCanvas::handleEvent` says
so -- and `Tab` is a key, so a canvas is the end of a window's tab ring rather
than a station on it. Without a way back the list would be a one-way door, and
`/` is the way back that a person coming from `less` or `vim` will try first.
-}
pressKey : Tui.Ports msg -> String -> Model -> Step msg
pressKey ports key model =
    when key is
        "Up" ->
            still (moveTo (model.cursor - 1) model)

        "Down" ->
            still (moveTo (model.cursor + 1) model)

        "PgUp" ->
            still (moveTo (model.cursor - onePage model) model)

        "PgDn" ->
            still (moveTo (model.cursor + onePage model) model)

        "Home" ->
            still (moveTo 0 model)

        "End" ->
            still (moveTo (Array.length (shown model)) model)

        "x" ->
            sendToHex model

        "X" ->
            sendToHex model

        "/" ->
            { model = model, command = Tui.focus ports findId, send = Nothing }

        "c" ->
            still { model | matchCase = not model.matchCase, top = 0, cursor = 0 }

        "C" ->
            still { model | matchCase = not model.matchCase, top = 0, cursor = 0 }

        "y" ->
            copyValue ports model

        "Y" ->
            copyShown ports model

        _ ->
            still model


still : Model -> Step msg
still model =
    { model = model, command = Cmd.none, send = Nothing }


scrollTo : Int -> Model -> Model
scrollTo where_ model =
    { model | top = clamp 0 (lastTop model) where_ }


{-| Move the cursor, and bring the window with it.

The cursor is an index into the *variables* and `top` is an index into the
*lines*, so the second is worked out from the first every time rather than
nudged alongside it. `firstLineOf` is the join between the two, and it is one
scan of an array the view is about to build anyway.

Scrolling is the minimum that puts the whole of the variable on screen: above
the top it scrolls to the variable's first line, below the bottom it scrolls so
the *last* of its lines is the bottom row. A value that wraps onto more lines
than the window has rows would then never fit, so the first line wins -- you
can see where it starts and page down through the rest, which is the same
answer `less` gives.
-}
moveTo : Int -> Model -> Model
moveTo where_ model =
    let
        count =
            Array.length (shown model)

        cursor =
            clamp 0 (max 0 (count - 1)) where_

        lines =
            laidOut model.cols (shown model)

        firstLine =
            firstLineOf cursor lines

        lastLine =
            firstLine + max 1 (linesOf cursor lines) - 1

        top =
            if firstLine < model.top then
                firstLine

            else if lastLine > model.top + model.rows - 1 then
                max firstLine (lastLine - model.rows + 1)

            else
                model.top
    in
    { model | cursor = cursor, top = clamp 0 (lastTop model) top }


firstLineOf : Int -> Array Line -> Int
firstLineOf at lines =
    when Array.findFirst (\line -> line.at == at) lines is
        Just found ->
            found.index

        Nothing ->
            0


linesOf : Int -> Array Line -> Int
linesOf at lines =
    Array.length (Array.keepIf (\line -> line.at == at) lines)


{-| How far `PgUp` and `PgDn` move the cursor.

Variables and not lines, because the cursor counts variables -- and *most*
variables are one line, so a page of rows is about a page of them. Counting the
lines the current page happens to hold would make the key move a different
distance depending on where it was pressed, which is worse than approximate.
-}
onePage : Model -> Int
onePage model =
    max 1 (model.rows - 1)


lastTop : Model -> Int
lastTop model =
    max 0 (Array.length (laidOut model.cols (shown model)) - model.rows)


{-| How many rows of a window this tall are the list.

Five are spoken for: two frame rows, the row the search box is on, the blank
under it, and the status line at the bottom.
-}
rowsIn : Int -> Int
rowsIn height =
    max 1 (height - 5)


{-| And how many columns of a window this wide.

Three: the left wall, the right wall, and the scroll bar -- except that the
scroll bar *is* the right wall, which is where the third one goes.
-}
colsIn : Int -> Int
colsIn width =
    max 20 (width - 3)


{-| `y`: the value under the cursor, and nothing else.

The value alone rather than `NAME=value`, because a copy of one is a thing you
are about to paste somewhere that wants the value -- a shell, a browser, the
search box of whatever is confused by it. The whole line is what a copy of
*many* wants, which is why `Y` still writes it that way: a list of bare values
with nothing saying which is which would be unreadable.

**An `Opaque` value refuses rather than copying its rendering.** What is on the
screen for one is dots, and dots are not what is in the variable -- a clipboard
holds text and there is no text here to hold. Copying the rendering would put a
plausible lie somewhere the user will paste it. `x` is the answer and the
message says so.
-}
copyValue : Tui.Ports msg -> Model -> Step msg
copyValue ports model =
    when Array.get model.cursor (shown model) is
        Nothing ->
            still { model | message = "Nothing to copy." }

        Just pair ->
            when pair.value is
                Opaque _ ->
                    still
                        { model | message = "That value is not text -- x sends it to the hex viewer." }

                Exact it ->
                    copying ports it model

                Reported it ->
                    copying ports it model


copying : Tui.Ports msg -> String -> Model -> Step msg
copying ports text model =
    { model = { model | copied = True, message = "" }
    , send = Nothing
    , command = Tui.copyToClipboard ports text
    }


{-| What is on the screen, as `NAME=value` -- one line each, unwrapped.

Unwrapped because a copy is for pasting somewhere else, and the wrapping is a
fact about this window rather than about the variable. Filtered because the
filter is the selection: there is no cursor in this list and no need for one,
since narrowing to the variable you want takes fewer keystrokes than walking to
it would.
-}
copyShown : Tui.Ports msg -> Model -> Step msg
copyShown ports model =
    let
        picked =
            shown model
    in
    if Array.isEmpty picked then
        still { model | message = "Nothing to copy." }

    else
        { model = { model | copied = True, message = "" }
        , send = Nothing
        , command =
            Tui.copyToClipboard ports
                (String.join "\n" (Array.map written picked))
        }


{-| One line of a copy of many.

An `Opaque` value is *named* rather than written, because what is on the screen
for one is dots and dots are not what is in the variable. `y` refuses outright
for that reason; `Y` cannot, since refusing a whole filtered set because one
entry is binary would be useless -- so it writes something that cannot be
mistaken for a value and says how much of it there is. Silently leaving the
variable out was the other candidate and is worse: a copy of everything shown
that quietly is not everything shown is the kind of wrong nobody checks.
-}
written : Pair -> String
written pair =
    when pair.value is
        Opaque bytes ->
            pair.name
                ++ "=<"
                ++ String.fromInt (Array.length bytes)
                ++ " bytes, not text>"

        _ ->
            pair.name ++ "=" ++ shownAs pair.value


{-| Hand the variable under the cursor to the hex viewer.

**Why this exists at all is the encoding.** A value that is not valid UTF-8 has
no rendering that answers "what is actually in there" -- the list shows the
printable bytes and dots, which says where the trouble is and not what it is.
EUC-KR, a latin-1 path, a stray NUL: all of them are dots. The hex viewer is
the thing that answers it, and it already takes bytes that were never a file.

`Reported` is the one value that cannot go, and the message says why rather
than the key doing nothing: on a machine with no `/proc/self/environ` predc
never had the bytes, only Node's decoding of them, and sending *that* would put
`EF BF BD` on the screen -- an answer, confidently wrong, to the question the
user is asking.
-}
sendToHex : Model -> Step msg
sendToHex model =
    when Array.get model.cursor (shown model) is
        Nothing ->
            still { model | message = "Nothing to send." }

        Just pair ->
            when bytesOf pair.value is
                Nothing ->
                    still
                        { model
                            | message =
                                "No raw bytes on this machine -- "
                                    ++ "/proc/self/environ is where they come from."
                        }

                Just bytes ->
                    { model = { model | message = "" }
                    , command = Cmd.none
                    , send = Just { name = "$" ++ pair.name, bytes = bytes }
                    }


-- VIEW


view : Inks -> Model -> Tui.Window
view inks model =
    let
        picked =
            shown model

        width =
            model.rect.x2 - model.rect.x1

        height =
            model.rect.y2 - model.rect.y1

        -- What the canvas is at the size the window was *built*. The model's
        -- `cols` and `rows` are what it is now, which is the same number until
        -- somebody drags the frame; these two lay the views out and those two
        -- decide what goes in them.
        wide =
            colsIn width

        tall =
            rowsIn height

        -- Right and bottom edges follow, and so does the left one, which is
        -- what keeps a view the same distance from the right-hand frame while
        -- it grows downwards. There is no name for this combination in `Tui`
        -- because only a scroll bar down the right-hand side wants it.
        alongTheEdge =
            { left = True, top = False, right = True, bottom = True }

        acrossTheFoot =
            { left = False, top = True, right = True, bottom = True }
    in
    { id = windowId
    , title = "Environment"
    , rect = model.rect
    -- Both directions. The height is what the list wants and the width is what
    -- a `PATH` wants: values wrap at whatever the window is, so widening it is
    -- worth doing and the tool has to notice when you do.
    , resize = Tui.resizable
    , palette = Tui.BlueWindow
    , canClose = True
    , canMove = True
    , views =
        -- The search box first, so it holds the caret when the window opens.
        -- Typing is what this tool is for, and the list is already drawn.
        [ InputLine
            { id = findId
            , rect = { x1 = 9, y1 = 1, x2 = min 46 (width - 24), y2 = 2 }
            , maxLen = 128
            , value = model.needle
            , allowed = Nothing
            }
        , Label
            { id = "env.find.label"
            , rect = { x1 = 2, y1 = 1, x2 = 9, y2 = 2 }
            , text = "Search"
            , for = findId
            }
        , Grows
            { -- Pinned to the right-hand frame rather than left where it was
              -- built, because a window this one is stretched to twice its
              -- width should not leave its check box marooned in the middle.
              grow = Tui.pinRight
            , view =
                CheckBoxes
                    { id = caseId
                    -- No `~` in the caption: it would claim an `Alt` letter for
                    -- as long as this window is open, and there are none going
                    -- spare. `c` on the list toggles it, and so does the menu.
                    , rect = { x1 = width - 21, y1 = 1, x2 = width - 3, y2 = 2 }
                    , items = [ "Match case" ]
                    , checked = [ model.matchCase ]
                    , available = []
                    }
            }
        , Grows
            { grow = Tui.stretch
            , view =
                Canvas
                    { id = listId
                    , rect = { x1 = 1, y1 = 3, x2 = 1 + wide, y2 = 3 + tall }
                    , lines = display inks model picked
                    , cursor = Nothing
                    , takesFocus = True
                    }
            }
        , Grows
            { grow = alongTheEdge
            , view =
                ScrollBar
                    { id = scrollId
                    , rect = { x1 = 2 + wide, y1 = 3, x2 = 3 + wide, y2 = 3 + tall }
                    , value = model.top
                    , min = 0
                    , max = lastTop model
                    , pageStep = model.rows
                    , arrowStep = 1
                    -- The list is the only thing in this window that scrolls,
                    -- so the wheel is taken from anywhere in it.
                    , for = ""
                    }
            }
        , Grows
            { grow = acrossTheFoot
            , view =
                Canvas
                    { id = "env.status"
                    -- To `1 + wide` and not past it: the scroll bar above sits
                    -- *on* the frame's right wall, and a status line as wide as
                    -- the scroll bar would rub the wall out on its own row and
                    -- leave the window looking torn.
                    , rect =
                        { x1 = 1
                        , y1 = 3 + tall
                        , x2 = 1 + wide
                        , y2 = 4 + tall
                        }
                    , lines = [ status inks model picked ]
                    , cursor = Nothing
                    , takesFocus = False
                    }
            }
        ]
    }


{-| The list, scrolled, with the needle picked out inside each row.

Every row on screen is a match while a needle is typed -- that is what
filtering means -- so marking whole rows would say nothing and would put
fifteen lines of inverse video on the screen to say it. Marking the *matched
text* answers the question the eye actually has, which is why this row is here:
`CMAKE_INCLUDE_PATH` and `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS` both match `path`, and only
one of them matches in its name.
-}
display : Inks -> Model -> Array Pair -> Array (Array Span)
display inks model picked =
    if Array.isEmpty picked then
        [ [ Tui.ink inks.dim ("Nothing matches " ++ quoted model.needle ++ ".") ] ]

    else
        laidOut model.cols picked
            |> Array.dropFirst model.top
            |> Array.takeFirst model.rows
            |> Array.map
                (\row ->
                    if row.at == model.cursor then
                        -- Padded to the full width so the cursor is a bar
                        -- across the list rather than a ragged patch the
                        -- length of whatever this variable happens to say.
                        lit inks
                            model
                            (\body ->
                                Tui.on inks.selected.bg (Tui.ink inks.selected.fg body)
                            )
                            (padTo model.cols row.text)

                    else if row.readable then
                        lit inks model Tui.plain row.text

                    else
                        -- A value that is not text, drawn as not text. The row
                        -- already reads `....` where the bytes are, and the
                        -- ink is what stops that being mistaken for a value
                        -- that genuinely contains dots. `x` is the answer to
                        -- it and the status line says so.
                        lit inks model (Tui.ink inks.alert) row.text
                )


padTo : Int -> String -> String
padTo width text =
    text ++ String.repeat (max 0 (width - String.count text)) " "


{-| One line, split into the needle and everything else.

Scanned character by character over the *original* text rather than by finding
an index in a lower-cased copy of it, which is the version that would be
subtly wrong: lower-casing is not always length-preserving (Turkish dotted
capital I becomes two code points), so an offset found in the copy does not
always mean the same place in the original. A line is at most seventy-six
characters and a needle is short, so comparing in place costs nothing worth
saving.
-}
lit : Inks -> Model -> (String -> Span) -> String -> Array Span
lit inks model base text =
    let
        chars =
            String.toArray text

        len =
            String.count model.needle
    in
    if len == 0 then
        [ base text ]

    else
        let
            slice from count =
                String.fromArray (Array.takeFirst count (Array.dropFirst from chars))

            same a b =
                if model.matchCase then
                    a == b

                else
                    String.toLower a == String.toLower b

            marked body =
                Tui.on inks.marking.bg (Tui.ink inks.marking.fg body)

            pushPlain body acc =
                if String.isEmpty body then
                    acc

                else
                    Array.pushLast (base body) acc

            go index plainFrom acc =
                if index > Array.length chars - len then
                    pushPlain (slice plainFrom (Array.length chars - plainFrom)) acc

                else if same (slice index len) model.needle then
                    go (index + len)
                        (index + len)
                        (acc
                            |> pushPlain (slice plainFrom (index - plainFrom))
                            |> Array.pushLast (marked (slice index len))
                        )

                else
                    go (index + 1) plainFrom acc
        in
        go 0 0 []


{-| The foot of the window: how many variables are in front of you, and then
the keys.

**Three colours and not one**, which is the whole of what changed here. The
line was a `StaticText` -- one run, one colour -- so the keys at the end of it
read as the tail of a sentence rather than as keys, in a window where they are
the only way to reach the list at all. It is a `Canvas` now: the count stays in
the window's own colour because it is an *answer*, and the keys go through
[`Theme.hint`](Theme#hint), which paints the name of each one louder than the
word beside it. That is what the status line along the bottom of the screen has
always done, and the reason `Alt-X Exit` down there reads as an instruction
while this line did not.

A message replaces the lot, keys included, because a message is what just
happened and the keys are always true.
-}
status : Inks -> Model -> Array Pair -> Array Span
status inks model picked =
    if not (String.isEmpty model.message) then
        [ Tui.plain model.message ]

    else
        let
            howMany =
                if String.isEmpty model.needle then
                    String.fromInt (Array.length model.all) ++ " variables"

                else
                    String.fromInt (Array.length picked)
                        ++ " of "
                        ++ String.fromInt (Array.length model.all)
                        ++ " match "
                        ++ quoted model.needle
        in
        [ Tui.plain
            (howMany
                ++ (if model.matchCase then
                        ", matching case"

                    else
                        ""
                   )
            )
        ]
            -- `Tab` is on the hint because the search box has the caret when
            -- the window opens and a focused canvas is the end of the tab
            -- ring: the one-letter keys belong to the list, and the way to the
            -- list is `Tab`. `/` is the way back.
            --
            -- `x hex` earns its four columns on an eighty-column terminal
            -- because it is the only way to see a value the list cannot show
            -- -- and the list says which those are, in the alert ink, so the
            -- hint has something on screen to be about.
            ++ Theme.hint inks "   ~Tab~ list   ~/~ search   ~c~ case   ~y~ copy   ~x~ hex"


quoted : String -> String
quoted text =
    "\"" ++ text ++ "\""
